WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.022 --> 00:00:10.912
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> Judi Dench is one of a golden generation of British actors

2
00:00:10.963 --> 00:00:19.996
who have had long and successful careers, including Maggie Smith, Vanessa Redgrave,

3
00:00:20.485 --> 00:00:27.511
Ian McKellen, Michael Caine, and Anthony Hopkins.

4
00:00:28.308 --> 00:00:38.113
She has acted in the theatre, on television, and in films in a career of nearly sixty years.

5
00:00:39.272 --> 00:00:43.621
Judith Olivia Dench was born on the ninth of December

6
00:00:43.659 --> 00:00:47.725
nineteen thirty-four in York in the north of England.

7
00:00:50.711 --> 00:00:57.550
Dench’s father was a doctor, but she had connections from a very early age to the

8
00:00:57.575 --> 00:01:03.141
Theatre Royal in York – her father was the theatre’s doctor

9
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.207
and her mother made costumes for the theatre.

10
00:01:10.745 --> 00:01:16.458
As a teenager, Judi Dench acted in the York Mystery Plays.

11
00:01:19.598 --> 00:01:27.396
These religious plays have been performed in York every four years since the fourteenth century.

12
00:01:29.583 --> 00:01:37.921
In the late nineteen fifties and nineteen sixties, she acted with Britain’s most important theatre companies,

13
00:01:38.153 --> 00:01:43.969
the Old Vic, the Royal Court, and the Royal Shakespeare Company.

14
00:01:45.243 --> 00:01:49.940
In the nineteen seventies and nineteen eighties, Dench continued

15
00:01:49.965 --> 00:01:53.684
to work in the theatre and on British television.

16
00:01:54.906 --> 00:02:02.241
In nineteen seventy eight, she acted in Shakespeare’s <i>Macbeth</i> with the actor Ian McKellen,

17
00:02:02.357 --> 00:02:05.316
both in the theatre and on film.

18
00:02:06.925 --> 00:02:11.866
Many people think it was one of the best ever performances of the play.

19
00:02:14.002 --> 00:02:20.281
She also appeared in British films like <i>A Room with a View</i> in nineteen eighty-five

20
00:02:20.487 --> 00:02:24.167
And <i>Henry V</i> in nineteen eighty-nine.

21
00:02:26.071 --> 00:02:31.630
In the nineteen nineties, her career changed with two films.

22
00:02:32.055 --> 00:02:36.172
The first of these was the James Bond film, <i>GoldenEye</i>,

23
00:02:36.443 --> 00:02:42.194
where Dench played Bond’s boss, M, normally a part for a man.

24
00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:46.737
Dench was a great success in the part.

25
00:02:47.045 --> 00:02:50.713
She was hard, and at the same time funny.

26
00:02:50.931 --> 00:02:58.304
She played the part in eight films, with both Pierce Brosnan and Daniel Craig as James Bond.

27
00:02:59.707 --> 00:03:06.064
The second film that changed her career was <i>Mrs Brown</i> in nineteen ninety-seven.

28
00:03:06.578 --> 00:03:09.087
Dench played Queen Victoria.

29
00:03:09.435 --> 00:03:17.670
This small, British film was a great success and she was nominated for an Oscar for Best Actress.

30
00:03:19.317 --> 00:03:27.012
In nineteen ninety-eight, she played Queen Elizabeth the first in the comedy, <i>Shakespeare in Love</i>.

31
00:03:27.629 --> 00:03:30.074
The film was another big hit.

32
00:03:30.306 --> 00:03:34.050
It won the nineteen ninety-nine Oscar for Best Film,

33
00:03:34.205 --> 00:03:38.554
and Judi Dench won the Oscar for Best Supporting Actress.

34
00:03:39.056 --> 00:03:46.403
She’s only in the film for eight minutes, but, as ever, she’s absolutely brilliant.